{%- comment -%}
  Per-section appearance: background, text colour, font and text size.

  Emits a <style> block scoped to this section's own wrapper id, so the
  settings apply to that instance only and two copies of the same section can
  differ. Everything is expressed by overriding the theme's own custom
  properties rather than by restyling elements, which means a section picks up
  the change wherever those properties are already used and nothing has to be
  re-plumbed per element.

  Only the two house faces are offered. The brand is Helvetica Neue and, where
  it is not Helvetica, JetBrains Mono — a free-text font field would just be a
  way to break that quietly.

  Every control is optional. Left at its default, no declaration is written at
  all, so a section that has not been touched costs nothing and inherits.

  Usage:  {% render 'section-style', section: section %}
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign bg = section.settings.bg_color
  assign fg = section.settings.text_color
  assign fam = section.settings.font_family
  assign scale = section.settings.text_scale | default: 100
  assign pad = section.settings.section_padding

  assign sel = '#shopify-section-' | append: section.id
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  THERE IS NO MOBILE ORDER SETTING, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.

  This snippet used to emit `order: N` inside a mobile media query so a section
  could sit somewhere different on a phone. It worked, and it was wrong: tab
  order and screen-reader order follow the DOM, not the visual box order, so
  the phone showed one sequence and read another. WCAG 2.4.3 and 1.3.2.

  The homepage now has ONE order, in templates/index.json, and both the eye and
  the keyboard follow it. If a section needs to move on a phone, move it in the
  template — and accept that it moves on the desktop too. That constraint is
  the point: an order that cannot be read is not an order.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- capture rules -%}
  {%- if bg != blank and bg != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' -%}
    background: {{ bg }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if fg != blank and fg != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' -%}
    color: {{ fg }};
    --non-fg: {{ fg }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if fam == 'mono' -%}
    --non-sans: var(--non-mono);
  {%- elsif fam == 'sans' -%}
    --non-mono: var(--non-sans);
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- unless scale == 100 -%}
    --non-type-scale: {{ scale | divided_by: 100.0 }};
  {%- endunless -%}
  {%- if pad and pad > 0 -%}
    padding-top: {{ pad }}px;
    padding-bottom: {{ pad }}px;
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- assign rules = rules | strip -%}

{%- if rules != blank -%}
  <style>
    {{ sel }} { {{ rules }} }
    {%- comment -%}
      Descendants that paint their own background would otherwise punch holes
      in a custom one. Only neutralised when a background is actually set.

      The direct-child rule is the important one. Naming .non-section and
      .non-shell only covered the sections that happen to use those wrappers —
      but most paint their OWN root instead: .non-poured carries
      var(--non-invert-bg), and every hero carries the page colour. Setting a
      background in the editor did nothing on those, because the thing sitting
      on top of it was still opaque.

      Direct children only, so genuine inner panels — a verdict box, a product
      card, a drawer — keep their fills. And background-color rather than the
      background shorthand, so a section with an image or a gradient does not
      lose it to a colour swatch it never set.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if bg != blank and bg != 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' -%}
      {{ sel }} > * { background-color: transparent; }
      {{ sel }} .non-section, {{ sel }} .non-shell { background-color: transparent; }
    {%- endif -%}
  </style>
{%- endif -%}
